  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday) \nIssued at 1019 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge will begin to shift slightly eastward through \nthe start of the extended package, while weakening, in response to a \nseries of disturbances rippling across the PacNW. Though heights \nwill be near stationary, surface winds will be persistent out of the \nsouth-southwest in response to lee cyclogenesis to our north. This \nwill allow thickness values to increase which will aid in afternoon \ntemperatures above seasonal standards through the remainder of the \nwork week. Expect afternoon temperatures to climb into the 80s and \n90s across the Caprock regions as mostly clear skies prevail. A few \nensemble members are still attempting to suggest a few isolated \nshowers and thunderstorms will be possible across our southern row \nof counties Thursday afternoon and evening as a disturbance tracks \nthrough the Big Bend. However, most deterministic guidance maintains \na dry forecast for the area with the ECMWF ensemble in agreement \nwith this. the outlier, in a shocking turn of events, being the GEFS \nsuggesting a plume of moisture will enter the low-lying Rolling \nPlains and southern South Plains by Thursday morning. If enough \nreturn moisture can enter the region, there is a chance for \nisentropic driven showers and thunderstorms may develop. However, \nconsidering the lack of forcing and the bulk of the moisture being \nto our south, this forecaster thinks that most NWP guidance may be a \nbit bullish considering the expected synoptic set-up. Will opt to \nkeep NBM non mentionable PoPs for the time being, though PoPs may \nneed to be updated if guidance changes. A brief and subtle cool-down \nis expected Thursday and Friday, with scattered high-clouds expected \nto linger through the day as moist-upslope flow develops. \nTemperatures will not be much cooler, maybe a degree or two, but \ndepending on how this forecast evolves, we could see even cooler \nafternoon highs. This \"cool down\" will be short-lived, with the \nreturn of warm highs by the weekend.",
